drake: the argument as an opener, and Map as a type expression (#44, #60)

PART 1 — THE ARGUMENT IS AN OPENER (#60), which brings the mods actors into the gate.

A `par` whose value is itself an application opens a `parameters` list of its own, and
the enclosing list's next `par` was the only thing that could close it. Both readings
are legal and no look-ahead settles it, so the argument BRACKETS ITSELF, exactly as a
dyn-with-body brackets its body:

    par = <name> [ <fn> parameters
      par <value>
      ... ]

That is the second opener under the one BRACKETS rule rather than a new rule, and as
with the dyn the `[` is also what tells a leaf argument from a block one — `par x`
opens nothing and so brackets nothing. It lifts the restriction that an argument OF a
chain call could not itself be a chain: the `]` ends it before the enclosing chain can
claim the next `.member`.

DrakeParseTest now walks a second corpus, the mods actors, under both gates — 10 types,
of which aerial/Input (three named arguments, each a two-call chain) is the case that
was blocked. Named arguments and call chains had until now been gated only in miniature
because the draco corpus carries neither.

terrestrial/Output is held out of gate 2 only. A TUPLE argument carrying an application
renders INLINE, and an inline `( … )` group is one token to the lexer, so the
application inside comes back as host-opaque text. The surface still round-trips
exactly. Unlike the nested argument list this is not an ambiguity — nothing inside the
group is visible to the parser at all — so brackets do not address it; a multi-line
tuple form would, and that is a surface question of its own.

Drake.expression gained the `=` case it had always been missing. It only ever appears
inside a `()` operand list, so nothing reached it until a corpus with named arguments
came under the gate. The multi-line surface spells that node `par = <name> <value>` and
the flat renderer spells it `<name> = <value>`; same tree, two spellings.

PART 2 — MAP IS A TYPE EXPRESSION, NOT A REFERENCE.

`Map[K, V]` in a valueType is now `{K, V}` in BOTH drake and json, and the Scala
spelling is produced by the target alone. Six valueTypes across Dictionary,
TypeDictionary, DomainDictionary and CLI.

This inverts the direction the pipeline ran. The JSON carried Scala's own type syntax
and the drake emitter converted AWAY from it (Seq[T] -> [T]), which put the host's
spelling in the normative corpus and made every other target pay to undo it. Now
Generator.scalaTypeExpression is the one place in the pipeline that knows a brace pair
is a map; nothing upstream of emission names it and nothing downstream sees a brace.
It is applied by targetTypes at the generate entry, beside TypeLoader.rooted — one
normalization point rather than the 48 sites that emit a valueType, so the next
constructor to cross is a change in one function and no site can be missed.

The brace family is one bracket at two arities: {T} a set, {K, V} a map, a map being a
set of pairs. drake already spelled the set that way, so this completes a notation
rather than inventing one. parseTypeExpression's brace case is now arity-split, which
is transitional and says so: the map is already neutral in the JSON and is carried
through, the set is still Set[T] there and is converted back. When the set follows,
both arities are carried through and the case leaves both converters. Until now a
two-member brace came back as `Set[K, V]` — a standing wrong answer nothing had
exercised.

WHY NOT GIVE Map A PACKAGE, which was the alternative and the one I was asked about.
It is the corpus's only package-less reference, and because derivationRef only
qualifies on a name collision, adding one would have changed no generated Scala at all
— purely normative corpus data. But drake.dlt already resolves this: name the
capability, not the host language. Writing `scala collection immutable Map` into the
normative corpus contradicts that and pins the JVM inside the thing #21 wants to
project to Haskell and Lean. Making Map a type expression dissolves the question
instead: there is no reference left to package.

WHAT THIS DOES NOT CLOSE. Dictionary's DERIVATION on Map is untouched, because
`derivation` holds TypeNames and not type expressions. So Dictionary reads
`from Map(K, V)` on line 1 while its own elements read `{K, V}` — one concept spelled
two ways in one file. That reference is also the corpus's only package-less one, and
the reason Dictionary is the ONLY type in either corpus that spells DracoType beside
another parent: every other multi-parent type reaches the root through its named
parent, and Map, being foreign, does not root. Three findings, one fix — derivation
admitting a type expression, GitHub #51.

The Map derivation cannot simply be deleted. `removed` / `updated` / `get` / `iterator`
are exactly Scala's four abstract Map members and exist BECAUSE of it; without it they
implement nothing and Dictionary stops being a map. It is constitutive, not incidental.
EOF

